     "date": "2026-08-20",
     "note": "*** CLOSE IT -- MEASURED FIXED, AND MEASURED TO HAVE HAD NIL GUEST HARM EVEN WHEN BROKEN. ***  Reading the fixed code cannot prove the row was ever a defect, so the seat built a discriminator on one property (t->interval == 1.0/t->freq) and compiled the SAME DRIVER against shipped timer.c and against `git show 39748e3:src/core/timer.c`:\n    shipped 020a672  12 rows, 0 fails GREEN     upstream 39748e3  5 fails RED\nThree things a reading could not establish: (1) the row WAS real -- 5 of 12 go red pre-fix, so not vacuous; (2) *** timer_add WAS NEVER THE DEFECTIVE PATH *** -- upstream already clamped before storing there, and only timer_update_frequency stored raw, so the row's title ('the timer core') names a function too broadly; (3) grep -rn '->freq' src/ outside timer.c returns NOTHING -- t->freq is read only by the early-out inside timer_update_frequency itself, which pre-fix held raw-vs-raw, so idempotence survived too.\n\n*** AND THE BRIEF HEADER I WROTE IS A WRONG RECORD THAT SPLIT THE PANEL. ***  It says 'the LAST THREE unpanelled rows, each filed by ONE seat, none ever panelled.' The ledger gives tfreq 29 ENTRIES ACROSS ALL FOUR PHASES FROM ALL NINE SEATS, and the brief's own body quotes fourteen of them. The real criterion was 'thin ASSESS cell', not 'never panelled'. Seats trusting the header saw an open unreviewed defect; seats reading the body saw a finished round. Both were reading the same packet.\n\ntfreq/n2/n4 carry IDENTICAL closure notes, the same three commits (863d238 b9daca1 cdc4849) and the same hold. They are ONE ROUND WEARING THREE NAMES, active only because nobody flipped the state.\n\n---\n*** PHASE CORRECTED 2026-08-20: this answer was first filed under `review` and belongs in `assess`. ***  The cluster brief it answers asked the assess questionnaire -- rank, drop/merge, fix-vs-document-vs-detector, reachable-vs-theoretical, smallest harm, witnessable at what rung, what the filing seats missed -- and none of that is a diff review. Fourteen rows carried the same mis-filing.\n\nIt was caught by precommit section H, which reported something else: 'tfreq CLOSED-WHILE-HELD, a round may not close while a stage awaits a seat.' True as stated, and the cause was not a missing seat -- opus5 HAD answered, at 1628 bytes, into the wrong phase. A gate that names a real problem by the wrong mechanism is still the gate earning its keep; the alternative was closing fourteen rows over holds that looked live.",
