   "witness": "none yet -- half 1 is a host-side attach-path mis-typing with a guest-visible consequence (the CD-ROM is absent from the bus); a witness needs a build. Half 2 is latent.",
   "state_note": "*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: TRUE, and THE ROW COVERS TWO DEFECTS OF DIFFERENT SEVERITY -- which is the shape that buries one when the other closes. Split before working it. ***\n\n**HALF 1 -- LIVE.** A small `.iso` (El Torito sized, ~1.44 MB) is typed `DISKIMAGE_FLOPPY` while `is_a_cdrom == 1`. The conjunction is the defect: the guest's SCSI/IDE probes miss it, so **the CD-ROM is not on the bus at all**. The host banner shows it (`FLOPPY CD-ROM ... 1440 KB`) which is why it reads as cosmetic and is not.\n\n**HALF 2 -- LATENT.** The `d:` prefix does not behave like `s:`/`i:` here. Not guest-visible; a prefix asymmetry. **It should NOT ride along as a second guest defect.**\n\n*** A FIX OWES THE ORACLE-CORRECTION ORDER, AND THE ROW THAT MAKES IT SO WAS VERIFIED HERE. *** `regress/diff_diskimage_parse.c:293` ships\n  `check(\"[RECORD] bare 1.44M *.iso is FLOPPY-typed (filed defect)\", ...)`\nand `regress/gate_offline.sh:1693-1694` gates on that record being present. **The shipped detector PINS TODAY'S WRONG TYPE on purpose**, and `:290` says so in its own comment (*'becomes a FLOPPY CD-ROM, doubly unreachable. Filed separately'*).\n\n> So the sequence is fixed by rule: **RE-PIN THE ROW FIRST, measure the committed build RED under the inverted expectation, THEN change the typing heuristic.** Doing it the other way round is observationally identical to fitting the test to the binary.\n\n**Candidate fix and its warrant, both already in the tree:** the heuristic gains `&& !d->is_a_cdrom` (a one-liner already written down in `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md`), and the transferred warrant is the existing `&& !prefix_d` guard on `.iso`/`.cdr` at `diskimage.c:1852`. `#418` already moved `d:`'s type assignment after the recalc, which does NOT rescue a small ISO -- worth knowing, because it looks like it should.\n\n"
  },
