    },
    {
     "seat": "fable5",
     "phase": "assess",
     "d": "✓",
     "date": "2026-08-15",
     "note": "PARTLY-FIXED - SYNC CACHE does fsync the base file (:925-926, #134 tag), so that third is STALE; the live third is unknown commands returning as handled with no sense (:1373-1381). SPLIT three ways.",
     "ev": "pipeline/evidence/_dispatches/fable5_assess_2026-08-15.md",
     "grade": "MANUAL"
    },
    {
     "seat": "opus5",
     "phase": "assess",
     "d": "✓",
     "date": "2026-08-15",
     "note": "PARTLY-FIXED - the row is three thirds and only one survives. STALE: SYNC CACHE does fsync (:925-926). MIS-STATED: no write past the allocation is possible (both commands round retlen UP BEFORE allocating); the real defect is the inverse - data_in_len over-reports, and :385 is a dead store overwritten at :386 so the descriptor advertises 44 bytes when 36 exist. REAL: the unknown-command arm leaves status NULL and dev_asc.c:1284-1287 pushes 0x00 GOOD.",
     "ev": "pipeline/evidence/_dispatches/opus_batchB_2026-08-15.md",
     "grade": "CONFIRMED"
    },
    {
     "seat": "fable5",
     "phase": "research",
     "d": "OK",
     "date": "2026-08-29",
     "grade": "CONFIRMED",
     "ev": "Agent seat (fable5) adjudication 2026-08-29; census, osiop:500-505, asc:1395-1400 and the raw-vs-call grep contamination all re-verified by the main loop against source",
     "outcome": "REFUTED-MY-CENSUS-STRENGTHENED-MY-CONCLUSION",
     "outcome_note": "return 0 refuted on consumer evidence rather than structure; my helper counts were contaminated by the comment header and definition; the ATAPI route is the WRONG witness channel, which inverts what I assumed.",
     "note": "VERDICT: **`return 0` IS REFUTED -- not merely a sketch, but a MEASURED-BY-READING\nBEHAVIOURAL REGRESSION on two controllers with ZERO benefit on the third.  The right shape\nis `diskimage__return_check_condition(xferp, d, 0x05, 0x20, 0x00)` PLUS the fall-through\n`return 1` -- the file's own refusal idiom at four shipped sites.**  Every claim below was\nre-verified by me against the source before recording.\n\n*** MY CENSUS WAS CONTAMINATED AND SO IS THE ROW'S.  Both counted the COMMENT HEADER and the\nDEFINITION as calls. ***\n\n    grep -c 'diskimage__return_default_status_and_message'          -> 25   raw\n    grep -c 'diskimage__return_default_status_and_message(xferp'    -> 23   CALLS\n    grep -c 'diskimage__return_check_condition'                     ->  5   raw\n    grep -c 'diskimage__return_check_condition(xferp'               ->  3   CALLS\n    grep -c $'^\\tcase '                                             -> 22   top-level labels\n\nSo **the row's \"the only one of 25\" is wrong under ANY unit** -- 25 is the contaminated raw\ncount.  The correct statement is **\"the only one of 20 TOP-LEVEL ARMS\"** (22 labels forming\n19 bodies, since MODE_SENSE/10, READ/READ_10 and WRITE/WRITE_10 share bodies, plus the\ndefault).  My own `~30` counted NESTED labels.  *A re-deriver caution worth keeping: \n`grep -E '^\\tcase'` silently matches NOTHING, because `\\t` is a literal `t` in ERE -- use\n`$'\\t'`.  I hit that exact trap on this file earlier today.*\n\n**THE STRUCTURAL CLAIM SURVIVES, verified arm by arm**: all 19 bodies call the status helper\non every completing path.  ONE QUALIFIER: at PATH level the two `return 2` first passes also\nset no status -- correctly, because the command is incomplete and the DATA_OUT re-entry sets\nit, and all three SCSI controllers re-call.  My return enumeration was exact.\n\n*** WHY `return 0` IS WORSE THAN A SKETCH -- three consumers, each read: ***\n  * **`dev_osiop.c:500-505`** -- `res == 0` sets **`d->scripts_running = 0`**.  A guest\n    probing one unsupported opcode **kills its own SCRIPTS engine**; no status phase ever.\n  * **`dev_asc.c:1395-1400`** -- `ok == 0` gives `STATE_DISCONNECTED` + `NCRINTR_DIS`.  The\n    guest sees a target DISCONNECT instead of CHECK CONDITION: retry/reset instead of\n    REQUEST SENSE.  **This also refutes the row's own state_note, which says asc does not\n    test for 0 at all** -- its select path does.\n  * **`dev_wdc.c:1528-1541`** -- the `res == 0` arm sets **no error bit**, and `status_byte()`\n    raises `WDCS_ERR` only from `d->error`.  So on wdc, `return 0` is guest-INDISTINGUISHABLE\n    from success-with-no-data.\n\n> **`return 0` helps no consumer and harms two.**  That is a stronger refutation than my\n> structural one, and it is the form a future round should cite.\n\n**THE FIX, WITH ITS WARRANT:** CHECK-CONDITION-then-`return 1` is ALREADY this file's refusal\nidiom at four shipped sites, and all four controllers demonstrably handle it today.  `d` is\nnon-NULL in the default arm (the NULL path returned earlier).  #417's latch semantics come\nfree: REQUEST SENSE reads `d->sense_key/asc/ascq` and the next success retires them.\n*The state_note's cited precedent is the WRONG one -- that shape exists only because `d` is\nNULL there and `check_condition` would dereference it; its own comments say `// ?` and `TODO`.*\n\n**MUST NOT BREAK:** (a) do NOT allocate `data_in` -- all three SCSI controllers pick the\nDATA_IN phase off `data_in != NULL`, and status-only completion is already TEST_UNIT_READY's\nshape; (b) leave the pre-switch not-connected `return 0` alone; (c) `est/` byte-identical\npropagation, shared file; (d) **the fix ALSO cures `mb89352halt`'s mechanism** -- `status_len`\nbecomes 1, so the PH_STAT read no longer trips 'read longer than buffer?' into\n`cpu->running = 0`, consistent with the standing ONE-ROUND-for-both ruling.\n\n*** THE ATAPI ROUTE IS REAL AND IS THE **WRONG WITNESS CHANNEL**, which inverts what I\nassumed when I raised it. ***  `dev_wdc.c:1515` does feed packet commands in, and `res == 1`\nwith no data gives PHASE_COMPLETED and no error bit.  **But `atapi_st->status` is read\nNOWHERE in dev_wdc.c** -- so an ATAPI witness stays RED after a dispatcher-only fix, because\nit witnesses the COMBINED dispatcher+wdc defect.  The discriminating channels for THIS fix\nare **asc** (ICCS fifo byte 0x00 -> 0x02, pmax rig) or **mb89352** (loudest pre-fix symptom\non record: the emulated CPU stops).  The wdc consumer repair is its own hunk in a different\nfile and should be filed separately -- the one-omission-in-one-shared-function warrant does\nnot stretch to it.\n\nRANKING: raises reachability BREADTH within the band, not harm class.  Keep rank 3.  Plausible\nbut UNMEASURED: MMC opcodes 0x46 (GET CONFIGURATION) and 0x4A (GET EVENT STATUS NOTIFICATION)\nare not among the 19 arms and period ATAPI attach code issues them routinely -- one flagged\nboot would settle it.\n\n**FILED, NOT FIXED (a sibling this round does not touch):** `SPACE`'s NESTED `default:`\n`fatal()`s and then FALLS THROUGH to the status helper, **telling the guest GOOD for an\nunimplemented SPACE type**.  Same false-success class, inside a KNOWN arm rather than the\ndefault one, so b120u's fix does not reach it.  b120r-shaped."
    }
   ],
   "hold": "R8 - the unknown-command half only",
   "witness": "none yet -- GUEST-REACHABLE and witnessable at rung 3/4, but building the probe needs a build, which the machine embargo forbids. First in line with the other build-blocked rows.",
   "state_note": "*** L4 WRONG RECORD, CORRECTED IN PLACE 2026-08-27. An adversarial opus pass REFUTED THE MECHANISM THIS ROW STATED BELOW. The defect is real; my explanation of it was false, and the true picture is four different wrong outcomes rather than one. ***\n\n**WHAT I GOT WRONG.** I wrote that `scsi_transfer_alloc()`'s `memset(p, 0, ...)` leaves a status BYTE of 0x00, which the guest reads as GOOD. **`status` is `unsigned char *` (`diskimage.h:196-197`)** -- so the memset leaves `status == NULL` and `status_len == 0`. **There is no zeroed status byte anywhere.** Nothing reads a 0x00 out of that struct. The conclusion happened to hold for one controller, for an entirely different reason.\n\n**WHAT SURVIVES, and it is the structural half:** the `default:` arm is the **only one of 25** that never calls `diskimage__return_default_status_and_message()` or `diskimage__return_check_condition()` -- verified by grepping every case/helper pair -- and it falls through to `return 1`, which the function's own header (`:263-266`) documents as the success token (`2` = expects DATA_OUT, `1` = otherwise ok, `0` = error).\n\n*** THE HONEST STATEMENT: ONE OMISSION, FOUR DIFFERENT WRONG OUTCOMES. ***\n  * **`dev_asc.c` -- guest IS told GOOD**, but via an explicit `else fifo_write(0x00)` fallback at `:1284-1292`, which `git log -S` places in the unmodified upstream 0.7.0 import, NOT a fork change. Right conclusion, wrong reason.\n  * **`dev_mb89352.c` -- REFUTES the 'told success' claim outright.** `data_in == NULL` sets `phase = PH_STAT` (`:284`); the guest's PH_STAT read takes `p = status (NULL), len = 0`, trips the `read longer than buffer?` arm (`:173`) and **sets `cpu->running = 0`**. The guest is not told anything -- the emulated CPU is STOPPED. **Filed separately as [[mb89352halt]].**\n  * **`dev_osiop.c:573-582`** -- `while (... && i < status_len)` with `status_len == 0` writes **no status byte at all**; the guest's destination memory keeps its prior contents.\n  * **`dev_wdc.c` (ATAPI)** -- `atapi_st->status` is read nowhere in the file; `res == 1` with `data_in == NULL` gives `PHASE_COMPLETED` with `WDCS_ERR` unset: a successful packet command.\n\n> **That is a BETTER case than the row originally made, not a weaker one** -- a single omitted completion step producing four distinct wrong behaviours across four controllers. But it is not the claim as written, and the claim as written was the one being ranked.\n\n**A SECOND CONSEQUENCE THE ORIGINAL RECORD MISSED:** the helper also RETIRES LATCHED SENSE (the `#417` fix, `:187-188`). The `default:` arm skips that too -- so an unimplemented command following a failed one leaves `sense_key/asc/ascq` latched, and a follow-up REQUEST SENSE attributes the OLD failure to the command that just ran. That is exactly the regression `#417` was written to remove, reachable by a different door.\n\n**FIX SHAPE, now pinned by the in-tree idiom at `:319-327`:** call the helper, set `status[0] = 0x02` (CHECK CONDITION), then `return 0`. Note `return 0` is **distinguishable on only two of four controllers** -- osiop stops the SCRIPTS engine, wdc completes with a latched warning, while asc and mb89352 do not test for 0 at all -- so the status buffer, not the return value, is what carries the refusal.\n\n--- the original record follows, kept so the correction is checkable ---\n\n*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: TRUE, GUEST-REACHABLE, and the highest-severity bare-title row worked so far. Established by reading; the fix idiom ALREADY EXISTS in this file. ***\n\n`src/disk/diskimage_scsicmd.c:1380-1388`, the dispatcher's `default:` arm:\n\n```c\ndefault:\n        debug(\"[ UNIMPLEMENTED SCSI command 0x%02x, disk id=%i\", xferp->cmd[0], id);\n        // exit(1);\n}\ndebug(\" ]\");\nreturn 1;\n```\n\n**Three things go wrong together:**\n1. it logs through `debug()`, which is verbosity-gated and normally invisible;\n2. **it never calls `diskimage__return_default_status_and_message()`** -- every implemented arm does (`:1212`, `:1257`, `:1298`, `:1340`, `:1347`, `:1376`);\n3. it falls through to **`return 1`, the same value a SUCCESSFUL command returns.**\n\n**And the status the guest reads is GOOD, which was checked rather than assumed:** `scsi_transfer_alloc()` ends with `memset(p, 0, sizeof(struct scsi_transfer))` (`diskimage_scsicmd.c:89`), and `dev_asc.c:326-330` frees and reallocates per command. Status 0x00 IS GOOD. **So an unsupported opcode is reported to the guest as a completed, successful command.**\n\n*** THE COMMENTED-OUT `exit(1)` IS THE TELL. *** This site was de-fatalised by commenting out the kill, which removed the death without adding an error path -- the incomplete form of the de-fatalisation this fork otherwise does carefully.\n\n*** THE FIX HAS A TRANSFERRED WARRANT ALREADY IN THIS FILE, found by grepping the tree for the existing idiom rather than reasoning from scratch. *** `diskimage__return_check_condition()` is defined at `:208` and already used for exactly this class at `:823-824` -- `0x05, 0x21`, ILLEGAL REQUEST -- and again at `:909`. **The correct refusal for an unknown opcode is CHECK CONDITION with ILLEGAL REQUEST / INVALID COMMAND OPERATION CODE, and the helper to do it sits three hundred lines above the defect.**\n\n**NOT FIXED: needs a build.** The detector must show the pre-fix build reporting GOOD for an unsupported opcode and CHECK CONDITION after -- and floor clause (b) needs the `return 1` reinstated verbatim. Both are measurements. Queued behind the embargo.\n\n",
   "note": "\n\n*** 2026-09-11 ASSESS (codex GPT-6 Astra at max, 12,054 b).  **DEFECT CONFIRMED -- and THE FILING MISIDENTIFIES WHERE GOOD ORIGINATES, which changes the fix.** ***\n\n**THE HANDLER SETS NOTHING.**  `diskimage_scsicmd.c:1380-1388` is:\n\n    default:\n            debug(\"[ UNIMPLEMENTED SCSI command 0x%02x, disk id=%i\", ...);\n            // exit(1);\n    }\n    debug(\" ]\" NL);\n    return 1;\n\nIt **sets neither status nor message, allocates no response, and leaves latched sense unchanged** -- there is no CHECK CONDITION and no GOOD here.  On a fresh ASC transfer the result is `return 1`, `status == NULL`, `status_len == 0`, `msg_in == NULL`.\n\n*** THE GUEST-VISIBLE GOOD COMES FROM THE CALLER, `dev_asc.c:1284-1287`: ***\n\n    if (d->xferp != NULL && d->xferp->status != NULL)\n            dev_asc_fifo_write(d, d->xferp->status[0]);\n    else\n            dev_asc_fifo_write(d, 0x00);\n\n**A NULL status is SUBSTITUTED with `0x00` -- GOOD** -- and `:1289-1292` does the same for the message, giving COMMAND COMPLETE.  *So the false success is a CALLER DEFAULT filling a hole the handler leaves, not a value the handler chooses.*  The row read it as the handler reporting success, which would have aimed the fix at the wrong statement.\n\n*** AND THE OBVIOUS FIX IS REFUTED: `return 1` MUST BE RETAINED. ***  The documented contract (`:263`) is `2` = DATA_OUT required, `1` = otherwise OK, `0` = error.  **`return 0` propagates failure to the outer controller handler, which DISCONNECTS and FREES the transfer** (`dev_asc.c:1395-1405`: zero sets `STATE_DISCONNECTED`, asserts the disconnect interrupt, resets STEP, frees and nulls `xferp`).  **That discards the transfer through controller failure handling instead of delivering the intended error completion.**\n\n> **So the repair is: KEEP `return 1`, and SUPPLY CHECK CONDITION status.**  *This is the sketch rule again -- a proposed edit is not a fix until it has been run against the enclosing function's real control flow, and here the control flow lives one file away in the caller.*\n\n**THE IN-FILE PRECEDENT IS REAL AND ITS ARGUMENTS ARE WRONG FOR THIS COMMAND.**  `diskimage__return_check_condition()` (`:208`) does allocate status and deliver sense, taking `(key, asc, ascq)` -- **but its existing call sites pass a READ-error tuple, which is not the right error for an unsupported opcode.**  The round owes the correct tuple, not merely the correct helper.\n\n*Also checked and correctly set aside: the missing-target path at `:319` DOES allocate CHECK CONDITION and return 0 -- but it runs BEFORE opcode dispatch, so it neither refutes the existing-target defect nor serves as its precedent.*\n\n**STATUS: the fix is now specified** -- keep `return 1`, call the helper with an unsupported-opcode sense tuple, and pin the oracle on the FIFO byte the caller writes rather than on the handler's return value."
  },
