    {
     "seat": "fable5",
     "phase": "review",
     "d": "OK",
     "date": "2026-08-29",
     "grade": "CONFIRMED",
     "ev": "ledger state flip only; no new evidence",
     "outcome": "CLOSED-ON-EXISTING-ADJUDICATION",
     "note": "CLOSED 2026-08-29.  **No new decision was taken here.**  The row already carried assess 9/9 with the full roster NAMED (agy, codex, deepseek, fable5, glm, grok, kimi, minimax, opus5), an explicit CLOSE verdict, and a fable5 adjudication of the same date recording it as CLOSURE-READY and NOT CLOSED.  Flipping the state is the only thing that was owed.  *** IT SAT IN `held` WITH AN ANSWERED QUESTION, which is the state-vs-substance mismatch this project has recorded before: a future reader who trusts states reads `held` as 'waiting', and that is false. ***  It is the unread-review shape one level up -- the seat work was done, delivered and never banked.  Found while triaging the 75 held rows for the owner: only THREE of them were closure-ready, which itself corrected a claim I had just made that 43% of the backlog was seat-blocked (only 2 of 75 carry a seat marker)."
    }
   ],
   "hold_question": "CLOSURE-READY, recorded 2026-08-29.  Assess is 9/9 with the full roster.  The verdict is **NO SURVIVING IN-TREE CALLER EXISTS, AND `exitsweep` CREATES NONE -- documented-only, WITH AN EXPLICIT REOPEN TRIGGER**, verified independently before recording because it is a CLOSE recommendation.  The witness field already reads `none -- TRUE but NOT OBSERVABLE`, which is the honest form for a defect established by reading.  NOT CLOSED HERE: the reopen trigger must survive into the closure text, or closing destroys the one thing that would bring it back.  (Prior question, provenance: A disk that fails to be added stays on the list. The hold reads 'no measurable effect until a caller survives'. QUESTION: is there ANY caller in the tree today that survives a failed disk add and then)",
   "witness": "none -- TRUE but NOT OBSERVABLE. Established by reading 2026-08-27: both call sites terminate the process on failure and nothing walks the disk list in between, so no witness at any rung can exist without first adding a caller that survives the error.",
   "state_note": "*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27, established by READING under the machine embargo -- no build needed, and none would help. ***\n\n**THE CLAIM IS TRUE, and the asymmetry is unmistakable rather than arguable.** `diskimage_add()` (`src/disk/diskimage.c:1631-2104`) links the new disk into the chain at `:1822-1830`. **Six `return -1` exits sit AFTER that link** -- `:1964`, `:1980`, `:1991`, `:2074`, `:2088`, `:2099` -- and not one of them unlinks `d`. The most reachable is the plain `fopen` failure at `:1984-1991`: a disk whose file cannot be opened is left on `machine->first_diskimage` with `d->f == NULL`.\n\n**What makes it clearly unintended rather than a design choice: the PRE-link failures clean up correctly.** `:1803` does `free(d)` before the link exists. So the function frees on early failure and leaks-onto-the-list on late failure -- the split falling exactly at the link point, which is the signature of checks added after the link rather than of a decision.\n\n*** BUT IT IS NOT OBSERVABLE, AND THE CENSUS IS COMPLETE RATHER THAN SAMPLED. *** `grep -rnw diskimage_add` over `src/` returns **8 references, of which exactly TWO are calls**:\n  * `main.c:707` -- on `< 0` prints `Aborting.` and `return 1` (`:708-709`), with NO disk cleanup between;\n  * `emul_parse.c:558` -- on `< 0` calls **`exit(1)`** (`:561`).\n**Both terminate the process immediately.** There is no debugger or runtime path that adds a disk to a live machine (0 hits in `src/core/debugger*.c`), so the stale entry cannot outlive the call.\n\n**The one second-order route was checked and is also closed:** a shutdown walker would dereference the half-built entry (`d->f == NULL`). `atexit` appears **once** in the whole tree -- `emul.c:913`, `atexit(fix_console)` -- which restores terminal state and never touches the disk chain. *That check is the reason this is recorded as latent rather than as a possible NULL dereference at exit.*\n\n**DISPOSITION: TRUE, LATENT, NOT FIXED.** Unlinking on the late failures is a few lines and would be tidier, but the addition licence wants a measured delta or a transferred warrant and there is neither: the entry is unreachable by construction, so no row could redden on it and no detector could be built that is not vacuous. **It becomes live the moment any caller survives a failed add** -- that is the trigger to re-open this, and it is the thing to check in any round that adds a runtime disk-attach path.\n\n"
  },
