     "note": "*** VERDICT: KEEP OPEN. The python half is PARTLY closed by a different tool; the directory half is UNCHANGED and now WORSE; and it MEASURED A FOURTH BLIND SPOT THE ROW DOES NOT NAME, which makes check D vacuous on the normal commit path. *** LINE NUMBERS: the row cites :155 and :92, and at HEAD they are :970 and :907 -- ~800 lines of drift with the TEXT IDENTICAL. It reconciled this explicitly as intra-file growth rather than the wrong-tree signature (which shifts arms and changes which cases exist). PYTHON HALF, split answer: check E is unchanged and can never see a python file, but section R's check_probe_wiring.py sees all 56 python artefacts. **They answer different questions** -- E asks whether a detector carries an identity guard, R asks whether anything runs it -- so the gap is real but narrower than recorded. For a python probe the identity-guard analogue is a gate-side `rows run` pin, **and nothing requires a new probe to have one**. *** THAT IS THE SAME HOLE AS `gateflr`, and one extension to check_probe_wiring.py closes both. *** DIRECTORY HALF, unchanged and BROADER than the row says: check C's prefix alternation is `(src|regress|man)`, so **the entire `tools/` tree is invisible** -- precommit_check.sh cannot cross-check a commit message naming ITSELF, or panel.sh, or any pipeline checker. The alternation predates the 2026-08-17 harness migration into git, so this is NEW, not a restatement. It tabulated nine representative strings; `regress/drive_guest.py`, `tools/...`, and bare filenames are all invisible. The false-green is still there verbatim: no match -> `good \"message cites no source paths\"`, an ok line about a message it could not read. *** THE FOURTH BLIND SPOT: CHECK D AUDITS A DIFFERENT CHANGE SET FROM EVERY OTHER SECTION. *** `$CHANGED` prefers `git diff --cached`; check D uses BARE `git diff -U0` -- unstaged only, always. Sections R and V both use BOTH and carry a comment saying they learned it the hard way. **So on the ordinary workflow -- git add, run precommit, commit -- `$ADDED` is EMPTY and both of D's sub-checks print green about a diff they never read.** Its negative control for that is elegant and uses material already in the tree: the two stale `.sh:NNN` citations it found in check_probe_wiring.py's docstring are exactly what a widened check must name, plus a correct `.sh:NNN` that must stay green so the check is not merely 'reject all shell citations'."
