   "note": "\n\n*** RULED 2026-09-11 (codex GPT-6 Astra at max, 16,319 b, complete).  **DO NOT PROMOTE TO ONE CLASS ROUND.**  The twenty-day-old question is answered, and the answer is that the question's own premise was wrong. ***\n\n> *\"The stated census measures CANDIDATES, not a defect class; the number of justified separate rounds is not derivable here.  Keep rounds grouped by DEVICE AND SEMANTIC CONTRACT, allowing multiple sites when the existing detector covers the same required behaviour.  **Neither '62 handlers' nor '20 on booted machines' determines the round count.**\"*\n\n**THAT IS THE DERIVER LESSON ARRIVING IN A NEW PLACE.**  This project has already measured that a census keyed on a CORRELATE returns a plausible number for the wrong set -- the latch census keyed on variable NAMES, wrong in both directions across three attempts.  Here the correlate is *\"the handler does not consult `len`\"*, which is a property of the CODE; membership in a defect class is a property of the CONTRACT the handler owes.  **62 is a count of candidates and was being read as a count of defects.**\n\n*** AND THE THREE ROWS I CALLED \"GRINDING THROUGH THE CLASS ONE INSTANCE AT A TIME\" HAVE LARGELY SHIPPED THEIR FIXES.  My premise was stale in all three. ***\n  * **`pcc2widthkills`** -- *\"the explicit width exits are GONE\"*.  Current code has **twelve write-only width guards using a decline helper**; reads copy the requested span when it fits the register array and otherwise zero-fill.  The one remaining `exit(1)` is in unsupported interrupt-line handling -- **a different predicate, not this class.**\n  * **`sgirekills`** -- names **unimplemented reads AT THE ACCEPTED WIDTH**, which is a different defect from the six width guards.  *\"The cited read exits have been replaced\"*: five reads are implemented from stored state and the TEX read declines.\n  * **`dev_sgi_re.c`'s `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_de)`** -- its unsupported-width arm **now announces a decline** and jumps past register processing and drawing to the read-completion tail.\n\n**So the expensive picture that motivated the question -- three high rows each paying full freight for one instance of a shared class -- is not what the tree currently holds.**  That does not make the question wasted: it makes the answer *no promotion*, on evidence rather than on caution.\n\n*** A PREMISE OF MY BRIEF WAS WRONG AND THE SEAT CAUGHT IT: `src/devices/dev_crime.c` DOES NOT EXIST -- ANYWHERE UNDER THE PROJECT ROOT, INCLUDING THE THREE 2021-VINTAGE COPIES. ***  I listed it as a permitted file.  **The CRIME device is `src/devices/dev_sgi_ip32.c`, `DEVICE_ACCESS(crime)`, and `crimewidthkill`'s own title says so in its first three words.**  I derived the filename from the row's ID and from `dev_crime_init()` instead of reading the title that named it -- the scope-transfer class in miniature, and the fifth instance in three days.\n\n*The seat did the right thing with it: reported the path absent, went looking, and found the `{4,8}` width selection at `dev_sgi_re.c:1169` -- correctly noting that this establishes **a different available site, not the identity of the CRIME queue item.**  A weaker answer would have silently audited the file it found and reported on the wrong device.*\n\n**WHAT THE RULING LEAVES OPEN, named rather than glossed:** the seat could not settle supported lane semantics without `thirdparty/mvme_pcctworeg.h`, nor the SGI alignment claims without `cpu_mips_instr_loadstore.c` and `cpu_mips_instr_unaligned.c`.  It flagged the in-code comments about those as **evidence references, not independently verified reachability results** -- which is the honest form, and is how they should be cited until someone opens those files."
  },
