     "note": "FLAGSHIP ADJUDICATION, queue item 7, batched. Badge `s` disclosed: same-model review of the main loop's records, not a two-family check. Re-verified every code claim by grep/read; boots and mutant builds attributed to the measure seat rather than re-run.\n* *** THE FINDING IS A FLAW IN THE LADDER IT WROTE THIS MORNING: THE RUNG ORDER AND THE PRIORITY ORDER DIVERGE. *** b120r's witness is rung 4 (an instrumented boot) while armbdt's is rung 3 (a probe), so the ladder's clause 'order items by the strongest witness that exists TODAY' puts b120r FIRST. That is wrong for scheduling: b120r's measured guest-visible symptom is NIL (told-98 and told-35 give byte-identical transcripts) while armbdt CORRUPTS GUEST DATA. RUNG MEASURES REACHABILITY-PROOF STRENGTH, NOT HARM. The carrier clause is corrected.\nCONFIRM #1, ready now, and the fix must ship in ONE COMMIT with its gate row. READ-verified the mechanism at cpu_arm_instr.c:1549 (fast path masks addr[1:0] -- the architectural answer) vs :1562/:1755 (slow path hands RAW addr to memory_rw).\n* READ-verified THE GATE BLIND-SPOT: arm_writeback_probe.py targets the SINGLE-REGISTER base writeback (#19/#357/#362); no committed probe EXERCISES arm_pop/arm_push. Its claim that a grep 'returns nothing' was overstated -- the main loop found one match, a COMMENT at regress/arm_endian_probe.py:20 -- but the conclusion holds and the 22/22-including-the-entry-mutant result is structurally consistent, because that probe cannot see this defect.\n* AND THE COMMENT MATTERS: arm_endian_probe.py is #378, THE SIBLING WITH THE POLARITY INVERTED -- for multi-transfers the WARM rows discriminate and the COLD rows are the already-correct controls, exactly opposite to the alignment case. armbdt's probe design should be checked against it before it is committed.\n* The rung-3 witness exists but is UNCOMMITTED (private tree). Promoting it into regress/ is part of the fix round, not a precondition to it."
