     "note": "*** THIS ROW'S HOLD QUESTION WAS ANSWERED IN FULL ON 2026-08-26 AND THE ROW NEVER MOVED.\nBoth parts, by the codex seat, verified against the LOCAL manual.  Discharged 2026-08-29\nafter I re-verified every citation myself rather than crediting it. ***\n\n**(a) WHICH PATH IS RIGHT -- ANSWERED: the masked fast/warm path is RIGHT; the raw\nslow/cold path is WRONG.**  `memory_rw` uses the raw byte offset and copies four bytes from\nthere, so a load can span two architectural words and an STM can overwrite part of the next.\n\n*** AND IT REFUTED THE PREMISE THE QUESTION ITSELF CARRIED. ***  The hold text says *'the\nrelevant statement is in LDR's own Alignment note around p. A4-44'*.  **That governs LDR's\nrotate-the-aligned-word rule and does NOT govern block transfers.**  The controlling text is\n**LDM(1) p. A4-37** and **STM(1) p. A4-190**.  Re-verified by me at HEAD, from\n`_scratchpad/ddi0100i.txt`:\n\n    :7954  \"For CP15_reg1_Ubit == 0, the Load Multiple instructions ignore the least\n            significant two bits of the address.\"\n    :14115 \"For CP15_reg1_Ubit == 0, the STM[1] instruction ignores the least significant\n            two bits of address.\"\n\n**The page numbers are DERIVED, not transcribed**: the footer `A4-36` sits at `:7910`, so\n`:7954` is on the following page A4-37; footer `A4-189` at `:14071` puts `:14115` on A4-190.\n*This is a citation read from the local manual, which is the standard this project adopted\nafter #357/#362/#365 shipped ARM page numbers that came from panel output.*\n\nAnd the seat's own structural argument is the stronger half: **if A4-44 applied, the fast\npath -- which aligns WITHOUT rotating -- would not be correct either.**\n\n*** THE FIX SHAPE IS NARROWER THAN IT LOOKS, AND THIS IS THE PART A FUTURE ROUND WILL GET\nWRONG: mask ONLY the addresses passed to the two slow `memory_rw` calls.  Masking `addr` at\nfunction entry would CORRUPT WRITEBACK**, because Addressing Mode 4 computes writeback from\nthe raw `Rn` and the code writes its running raw `addr` back.  The generated optimised path\nconfirms the intended shape independently: select the page from the raw address, mask the\naccess offset with `addr &= 0xffc`, update the base register from the UNMASKED value.\n\n**A QUALIFICATION THE RECORD MUST CARRY, and the seat volunteered it rather than ratifying:**\n`ARM_CONTROL_ALIGN` is initialised ENABLED but is never enforced by any memory access.  So\nthe record may NOT say unqualifiedly that the fast path is *the architectural answer* -- it is\nthe answer for the emulator's implemented A=0/U=0 behaviour, and **at the advertised A=1 state\nBOTH paths are wrong**, because an alignment exception is owed and neither raises one.\n\n**(b) RANKING -- ANSWERED: above `b120r`.**  And the question's own framing was wrong too:\n`fable_queue.md` ALREADY ranks this first among the five triage rows, so it was never sitting\nbehind boot-witnessed items.  The seat declined to generalise, correctly: this does NOT put it\nabove every boot-witnessed durability-loss or host-death defect, which need individual harm\ncomparisons.\n\n*** THE ONE THING STILL GENUINELY OWED, and it is a DURABLE-ARTIFACT gap rather than a\nreasoning gap: the rung-3 probe is PRIVATE AND UNCOMMITTED. ***  The seat could not certify\nit in a read-only session because no artifact exists under `regress/`.  So its endorsement of\nthe WITNESS is READ-from-record while the manual-and-source finding stands on its own -- and\nthe row's `witness` field being empty is CONSISTENT with that, not an oversight.  **A\nreproduction whose artifact lives nowhere durable cannot be re-verified by anyone**, which is\nthe volatile-path rule aimed at a probe instead of a log.\n\n*Recorded because the answer existed for three days in an entry nobody had acted on.  This is\nthe unread-review class -- the most expensive shape this project has -- caught by reading the\nrow's own entries before starting new work on it.*"
