        "witness":  "harness:MEASURED by running check_probe_wiring.py at --today 2026-09-19 (PASS) and --today 2026-09-21 (FAIL, 8 problems)",
        "note":  "*** EIGHT DATED EXEMPTIONS EXPIRE ON ONE DAY, AND THE CHECKER HARD-FAILS.  MEASURED, NOT REASONED. ***      python tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py --today 2026-09-19   -\u003e  PROBEWIRING_PASS  (46/54 gated, 8 dated-exempt)\n    python tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py --today 2026-09-21   -\u003e  PROBEWIRING_FAIL  8 problem(s)\nSo **every commit after 2026-09-20 is blocked** until all eight are wired or the dates are moved.  The checker is right to do this -- a dated exemption that never comes due is not an exemption -- but eight coming due together is a scheduling hazard rather than a deadline.  *** AND THE POPULATION IS TWICE WHAT THE REGRESS REVIEW NAMED. ***  That review found *\u0027FOUR wdc probes share the single-leg-mode debt and the same 2026-09-20 expiry\u0027* and recommended ONE design round covering four instruments.  Correct about the wdc cluster; the expiry itself is shared by EIGHT probes with FOUR DISTINCT remedies:  **(1) single-leg mode, 4 probes** -- `wdc_altoffset_probe.py`, `_sig_`, `_4bit_`, `wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py`.  Two-leg differentials; a gate has only one binary.  Two of them additionally need a CD-ROM rig (`ciR:`) and one needs a POISONED image the battery does not build.  This is the one-design-round cluster.  **(2) `hpcmips_ctor_probe.py`** -- rung 2, ~8 s, no rig image; wants a CONSTRUCTION gate.  Different mechanism entirely.  **(3) `sh4_bsc_width_probe.py` -\u003e gate 10; `pit8253_latch_probe.py` -\u003e gate 14.**  Each names the gate it belongs in; these are wiring, not design.  **(4) `fbpending_drain_probe.py`** -- REPORT-ONLY until `EXPECT_CAP` is set, so wiring it without setting that ships a row that cannot fail.  *** THE POINT IS THE SHAPE, NOT THE COUNT: four of the eight are CHEAP (two are plain gate wiring, one is a one-variable decision), and only the wdc four need a design round. ***  Doing the cheap four first shrinks the cliff from eight to four before it arrives, and each is independently shippable.  *Advisory, from the same run: the checker also notes two SELFLABEL discrepancies (`pit8253_latch_probe.py` and `wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py` -- manifest says detector, the probe\u0027s own summary line says witness).  It flags these as advisory precisely because self-labels are measurably unreliable in both directions.*  *** ONE OF THE EIGHT WAS ABOUT TO BE GATE-WIRED WITH NO WAY TO DETECT A TRUNCATION.  FIXED PRE-EMPTIVELY 2026-08-30. ***  `pit8253_latch_probe.py`\u0027s last output was a bare `PASS`/`FAIL` -- a word that occurs in dozens of contexts and is not uniquely greppable.  Its only SCREAMING_TOKEN was `print(\"MEASURED\")`, a **mid-run section header** that a truncated log still contains.  So a gate row written against it could not have distinguished a completed run from a killed one, which is exactly the `killdrivercrash` class arriving in new work rather than old.  Now emits `PIT8253_LATCH_DONE`, on both the pass and fail paths -- a completed FAILURE is not a truncation.  *Doing this BEFORE the wiring means the gate row can be written correctly the first time.  `sh4_bsc_width_probe.py`, the other plain-wiring member, already carries a proper `SH4_BSC_WIDTH_PASS`/`_FAIL` pair and needs nothing.*  *** AND A METHODOLOGICAL CORRECTION WORTH MORE THAN THE FIX, because it nearly became a filed defect count. ***  A sweep for probes lacking a `_DONE`/`_PASS` token returned **34 of 46**.  That number is MEANINGLESS: gate 10 verifies `sh_rounding_probe.py` by grepping `SH_ROUND_RESULT=`, which is a perfectly good completion marker in a different shape.  The convention is per-probe and per-gate, so **there is no 34-probe defect and the question is not sweepable** -- it is \u0027does this probe\u0027s CONSUMER verify completion, and by what?\u0027, one probe at a time.  An earlier version of the same sweep put `sh4_bsc_width_probe.py` in the missing list, which is how the pattern was caught: it flagged a probe I had confirmed compliant ten minutes earlier.  **A pattern that selects the wrong set produces a clean, confident, wrong population**, and this is the third instance today.  *** THE CHEAP FOUR, INVESTIGATED READ-ONLY 2026-08-30 SO A ROUND CAN START WITHOUT RE-DERIVING ANY OF IT.  Ranked by what each actually needs. ***  **(a) `pit8253_latch_probe.py` -\u003e gate 14.**  ALREADY UNBLOCKED: it lacked any completion marker and now emits `PIT8253_LATCH_DONE`.  Machine `-E cats`, 10 rows.  Gate 14 is `gate_arm.sh`, which already runs `-E cats`.  Remaining work is the exec line plus row checks.  **(b) `sh4_bsc_width_probe.py` -\u003e gate 10.**  Machine `-E landisk`; already emits `SH4_BSC_WIDTH_PASS`/`_FAIL`.  **Gate 10 (`gate_sh_rounding.sh`) already execs three probes in exactly the needed idiom** -- `python3 \u003cprobe\u003e.py \"$PMAX\" \"$KERNEL\" \u003e \"$LOG\" 2\u003e\u00261 || true`, then `check` rows against the log.  Copying that idiom is the whole change.  **(c) `hpcmips_ctor_probe.py` -\u003e a construction gate.**  Rung 2, ~8 s, NO RIG IMAGE, takes no positional argv, accepts `--binary PATH` (default `$GXROOT/build/gxemul`), emits `HPCMIPS_CTOR_PASS`/`_FAIL`, and already runs its subprocess with a timeout.  `gate_asan_sweep.sh` is the construction gate and sweeps 41 machine types.  ONE MISMATCH TO RESOLVE, named rather than discovered later: that gate drives an ASAN binary while this probe defaults to `build/gxemul` -- `--binary` makes it parameterisable, so the decision is which binary the row should assert against.  **(d) `fbpending_drain_probe.py` -- ONE VARIABLE, BUT NOT A FREE ONE.**  It is REPORT-ONLY until `--expect-cap N` is set (`EXPECT_CAP = -1` means report only), and **wiring it without choosing N ships a row that cannot fail** -- the check-that-cannot-fail shape.  Choosing N needs a measurement, and the probe carries a **31.51 s wall-clock floor by construction**, so it is timing-sensitive and must be measured on a quiet machine.  Cheapest in code, most expensive in care.  *Ordering follows from that: (a) and (b) are mechanical, (c) needs one decision, (d) needs a measurement.  Three of the four can ship before the wdc design round even starts.*  *** `8404520` NAMED: that commit shipped this row\u0027s FIRST piece of code -- the `PIT8253_LATCH_DONE` sentinel -- and the attribution check refused the NEXT commit until the row said so.  Third time today this rule has fired (after `census` and `wdcatapistswap`), and every one was a real gap rather than a nuisance. ***  The row stays OPEN: one of eight probes is unblocked, seven remain, and the cliff is unmoved until they are wired.\n\n[MAIN-LOOP NOTE, 2026-09-09, phase assess -- moved out of the seat entries because the dashboard declares no such seat]  RE-DERIVED FROM THE CHECKER, and three of this project\u0027s own records about this row were wrong.  (1) The exemption set is what `grep -n 2026-09-20 check_probe_wiring.py\u0027 returns, and the checker states the same count in its own comment -- the ledger TITLE said eight.  (2) The comparison is `d \u003c today\u0027, so the block is valid ON its stamped date and first reds the NEXT day; CHECKPOINT.md said it fails on the stamped date.  (3) Of the six, only fbpending_drain and hpcmips_ctor have a measurement-driven nonzero exit; the four WDC harnesses end in a bare `main()\u0027 or fall off the end after writing a table file.  CHECKPOINT.md said three.  *** SO WIRING THE FOUR AS THEY STAND WOULD SHIP FOUR GATE SECTIONS THAT CANNOT FAIL -- this project\u0027s own named class, four at once, installed BY the deadline. ***  AND A DERIVER OF MINE MEASURED A CORRELATE while correcting someone else\u0027s count: `grep -cE \u0027exit\\([1-9]\u0027\u0027 scored three of the four as failable, matching two `os._exit(127)\u0027 calls in forked pty CHILDREN (which give the PARENT no failure status) and one COMMENT quoting a removed call.  The class is \u0027does the parent exit nonzero when a row fails\u0027; the regex tested the TEXT.  Honest deriver: `sys\\.exit\\(\u0027 plus reading how each file ENDS.\n\n[MAIN-LOOP NOTE, 2026-09-09, phase assess -- moved out of the seat entries because the dashboard declares no such seat]  *** THE DISPOSITION IS AN OWNER DECISION AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN. ***  Every one of the six reason strings names a prerequisite that does not exist TODAY and is still true: an unset EXPECT_CAP (\u0027wiring is a design step first\u0027), a construction-gate home, a single-leg mode for the two-leg differentials, a ciR: CD-ROM rig and a poisoned CD image \u0027the battery does not build\u0027.  So the 2026-09-09 adjudication\u0027s \u0027four cheap exemptions as a small side-commit regardless of ranking\u0027 IS NOT EXECUTABLE -- it inherited the wrong title.  The useful split is not cheap/expensive but CONTROLLABLE (fbpending, hpcmips -- blocked on decisions we make, and both already exit nonzero) vs INFRASTRUCTURE-BLOCKED (the four WDC, needing an instrument mode AND rig images, and none of them failable).  Options: (a) re-date carrying each blocker\u0027s evidence, or (b) six design rounds.  If (a), each entry gains the owing round\u0027s id -- the checker\u0027s own standard is \u0027an EXEMPTION naming the round that owes it.  Debt that expires, not debt that disappears\u0027, and a bare re-date converts (a) into debt that disappears.",
        "commits":  "8404520 eec0f4a 8d038908",
        "state_note":  "[2026-10-18, #566] regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/ip30_abort_probe.py is wired into gate 14 (regress/gate_arm.sh, 41 checks) and its 2026-10-30 exemption deleted.  Next due: alpha_tbi_probe.py 2026-10-22 and lca_badval 10-23, lca_widewrite 10-26, lca_isalane 10-27 (the other session\u0027s), then the four wdc_* probes 11-05 .. 12-03.  The `ip30_abort_probe\u0027 entry in the due list below is history.  ||  *** STATUS 2026-10-18 (#565): ppc_rldicl_probe.py WIRED into gate_ppc.sh (gate 13) and its exemption DELETED, five days before it would have refused every commit (dated 2026-10-22; `d \u003c today\u0027).  Gate 13 PASS 140 checks, the rldicl block\u0027s 30 among them (regress/artifacts/ppcrldiclzero/gatewire_20261018/gate13/).  NEXT DUE: alpha_tbi_probe.py 2026-10-22 -- refuses from 10-23; the other session\u0027s Alpha area, so its gate block or an owner re-date is owed; then lca_badval 10-23, lca_widewrite 10-26, lca_isalane 10-27, ip30_abort_probe 10-30 and the four wdc_* probes 11-05..12-03.  THIS ROW STAYS OPEN. ***  ||  *** STATUS 2026-09-18 (GXEMUL): #536 SHIPS -- mk48_nvram_probe.py (57 rows) and hpcmips_ctor_probe.py (28 construction IDs) wired into gate_arm.sh; the other eleven 2026-09-20 exemptions re-dated to 2026-09-27 BY OWNER RULING; the selftest\u0027s M5a/M5b days DERIVED from the table, M5a ON the stamped date (the old literals: 21/22; a d \u003c= today mutant: 21/22).  Pass 2 + §F2, nine seats, fable5 LAST: SHIP.  THIS ROW STAYS OPEN: the eleven fall due again as ONE block on 2026-09-28 -- `exemptcliff` owns that; FILED from the round: hpcmipsc2, exemptcal, m5dayb. ***\n\n\n\n**RESIDUAL-REMAINS.**  `8404520` added PIT\u0027s completion sentinel, and PIT plus SH4 BSC were\nsubsequently wired.  **Six exemptions remain, all dated 2026-09-20, and the checker rejects them\nfrom the 21st**: `fbpending_drain_probe.py`, `hpcmips_ctor_probe.py`, the three `wdc_altoffset*`\nprobes, and `wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py` -- *which is the carrier for [[wdcaddtoinbufbound]]\u0027s proposed\nclosure, so the two rows are coupled.*\n\n*** THE ROW IS FRAMED AS \"WIRE SIX PROBES\" AND THAT IS THE WRONG JOB.  FOUR OF THE SIX CANNOT SERVE\nAS GATE ROWS AT ALL, AND WIRING THEM ON THE DEADLINE WOULD SHIP FOUR GATE SECTIONS THAT CANNOT FAIL.\nFound by a codex ANALYSIS job 2026-09-08, VERIFIED here. ***\n\nThe cliff is real and was reproduced first: `check_probe_wiring.py --today 2026-09-21` gives\n`PROBEWIRING_FAIL  6 problem(s)`, and `--today 2026-09-19` passes.  The comparison is `d \u003c today`,\nso the exemptions are good THROUGH the 20th and rejected from the 21st.\n\n**codex\u0027s finding, which the brief did not ask for:** *\"the signature and 4-bit probes print\nmeasurements without enforcing a pass/fail result ... All four WDC harnesses can finish with exit\nstatus zero after reporting failures.\"*\n\n**MEASURED here, per probe -- count of nonzero-exit paths and of PASS/FAIL tokens:**\n\n    fbpending_drain_probe.py       nonzero-exit 2   tokens PASS/FAIL      ENFORCEABLE\n    hpcmips_ctor_probe.py          nonzero-exit 3   tokens PASS/FAIL/_RESULT=   ENFORCEABLE\n    wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py        nonzero-exit 0   token  FAIL only      *** CANNOT FAIL ***\n    wdc_altoffset_probe.py         nonzero-exit 0   token  FAIL only      *** CANNOT FAIL ***\n    wdc_altoffset_4bit_probe.py    nonzero-exit 1   NO pass/fail token    partial\n    wdc_altoffset_sig_probe.py     nonzero-exit 0   NO pass/fail token    *** CANNOT FAIL ***\n\n\u003e **THREE OF THE SIX HAVE NO NONZERO-EXIT PATH WHATEVER, AND TWO OF THOSE EMIT NO RESULT TOKEN\n\u003e EITHER.  A gate section calling them greps for nothing and tests an exit status that is always 0 --\n\u003e the CHECK-THAT-CANNOT-FAIL class, in its purest form.**\n\n*** AND THE DEADLINE IS WHAT MAKES THIS DANGEROUS RATHER THAN MERELY UNTIDY. ***  On 2026-09-20 the\nchecker starts blocking commits, and the obvious way to unblock it is to add six gate invocations.\n**Four of those would be inert, and the checker would then go GREEN -- it verifies that a gate CALLS\nthe probe, not that the call can fail.**  The cliff would have converted a loud, honest red into a\nquiet, permanent false green, and nothing downstream would ever say so.\n\n*** THE DISPOSITION QUESTION IS THE MANIFEST CLASS, NOT THE WIRING, AND THE CHECKER ITSELF ALREADY\nSAYS SO. ***  Its own output carries:\n\n    note  SELFLABEL  wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py: manifest says detector, its own summary line says\n                     witness.  Advisory only -- self-labels are measurably unreliable in both\n                     directions.\n\n**A WITNESS reproduces a defect on a pre-fix build and is done; a DETECTOR must run forever and must\nbe able to go red.**  Four of these six behave like witnesses or measurement harnesses, and the\nmanifest classes all six as detectors.  So the candidate dispositions are:\n\n  1. **wire the two that enforce** (`fbpending_drain`, `hpcmips_ctor`) -- cheap, and codex names\n     plausible homes: `gate_arm.sh` already supplies the CATS stub for the first,\n     `gate_asan_sweep.sh` covers machine construction for the second;\n  2. for the four, EITHER give each a real verdict line and a nonzero exit -- which makes them\n     detectors in fact rather than by declaration -- OR reclassify them in the manifest, which is a\n     records change with no gate cost;\n  3. NOT: extend the dates.  That is the option the cliff exists to prevent.\n\n**Two corrections to the brief\u0027s own premises, both from codex and both checked:** `gate_wdc.sh`\nDOES NOT EXIST (I assumed it might), and `run.sh` runs **16** gates, none of which invokes any of the\nsix -- `gate_hygiene`\u0027s references to them are source-TEXT inspections, not invocations.\n\n*** WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, said plainly: codex could not run the checker (outside its allowlist),\nso it confirmed the six expiring ENTRIES without independently reproducing \"exactly six problems\".\nI did run it, and got six.  Its timings are source-based estimates and are NOT measured runs -- so\nthe cost side of \"four are cheap\" remains an estimate, and the row should not repeat it as measured.***  *** MEASURED 2026-09-17 BY THE OPUS 5 SEAT, ON A BATCH THAT WAS NOT ASKED ABOUT WIRING -- AND IT IS THE SHARPEST ANSWER IN THAT BATCH TO \"what is the smallest edit that breaks the fix and still passes?\" ***  For four of the nine rows reviewed, **the answer is THE ENTIRE FIX, REVERTED, and the row that stays GREEN is every row in the full battery.**     lca_confbus_probe.py      NOT REFERENCED BY ANY GATE    (lcashiftub)\n    lca_confwidth_probe.py    NOT REFERENCED BY ANY GATE    (lcaconfwidth, MED)\n    alpha_pfnoffset_probe.py  NOT REFERENCED BY ANY GATE    (alphapfnoffset, **HIGH**)\n    alpha_harness_probe.py    NOT REFERENCED BY ANY GATE    (alphaswpctxptbr, **HIGH**)\n  -- against the wired controls in the same batch: sh_iprbcd_probe.py, sgi_re_rgb_probe.py, gdrom_default_probe.py and diff_wdc_identify.c all ARE referenced.  `run.sh` runs an explicit `GATES=(...)` list and the four appear nowhere outside `regress/` and the wiring checker itself. *** TWO OF THE FOUR ARE HIGH-SEVERITY ROWS.  Delete `| (vaddr \u0026 ((1ULL \u003c\u003c pageSizeShift) - 1))` from `memory_alpha.c` and a HIGH page-walk defect is back with a CLEAN BATTERY. *** **THE SEAT EXPLICITLY DECLINED TO CLAIM THIS AS NEW, which is the right call and is why it lands here rather than as a fresh row:** `check_probe_wiring.py` already classifies all four as `CLASS_WITNESS` -- correctly, by the ladder\u0027s witness/detector timing line -- and its own entry for `lca_confbus_probe.py` says *\"but this probe RUNS IN NO GATE, so the coverage exists and is never executed: wiringcliff\"*. *** BUT THAT CHECKER IS UNCOMMITTED, so **nothing in any committed tree reports this today.** ***  The four closures are HONEST -- their `witness:` says `probe:`, which is exactly what it is -- and what is absent is STANDING DEFENCE.  The seat\u0027s disposition, adopted: this belongs in each round record as a DISCLOSED RESIDUAL on those four rows, not only as one filed id, because a reader of `alphapfnoffset` alone cannot see it.  *** SCOPE NOTE FROM THE FABLE ADJUDICATION, 2026-09-17: MAKE SURE THE FOUR `CLASS_WITNESS` PROBES ARE IN THIS ROW\u0027S SCOPE, NOT ONLY THE DATED EXEMPTIONS. ***  This row\u0027s own title is about the EXEMPT table expiring, and the four unwired witnesses (`lca_confbus_probe.py`, `lca_confwidth_probe.py`, `alpha_pfnoffset_probe.py`, `alpha_harness_probe.py`) are a different population -- classified `CLASS_WITNESS`, never exempted, simply never run. *** AND A TRAP THAT WOULD BITE THE FIX: `lca_confwidth_probe.py` MUST NOT BE WIRED AS-IS. ***  It scores **2/6 at HEAD** (measured 2026-09-17, `regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/head_20260917/`) because `#518` replaced the IOC_CONF kill with a decline.  Wiring it produces a PHANTOM RED; the remedy there is RE-PIN OR RETIRE, under the oracle-correction rule\u0027s order -- re-pin first, measure the committed build red under the new expectations, then change anything. **AND THE COMMITTED TREE ALREADY KNOWS**, which sharpens the gap rather than excusing it: `check_probe_wiring.py` carries all four as `CLASS_WITNESS` with their scores.  *The gap is the FAILING, not the knowing* -- the checker deliberately does not fail on that class.  *** OWNER DECISION 2026-09-17: STAGGER -- wire the cheap ones, re-date the rest with SEPARATED deadlines and the owing round named on each.  A blanket extension was declined for the reason this checker\u0027s own header gives: debt that expires, not debt that disappears -- and the present cliff exists because ONE deliberate alignment of two #442 debts was then copied by nine later exemptions. ***  THE ELEVEN ARE NOT ONE JOB.  Derived 2026-09-17 by reading each probe rather than its exemption text, and they fall into FIVE classes: (1) THREE ALPHA PROBES -- alpha_tbi, lca_badval, lca_isalane -- share ONE blocker; (2) FOUR wdc_* probes are TWO-LEG DIFFERENTIALS needing a single-leg mode, and two of those additionally need rig images the battery does not build; (3) fbpending_drain is REPORT-ONLY until EXPECT_CAP is designed; (4) mk48_nvram needs an mvme187 leg on gate_m88k_rounding.sh; (5) ppc_rldicl lacks the RESULT/CONTROL protocol AND is on the do-not-commit list, so it CANNOT be wired at all and must be re-dated whatever else happens.  *** THE FINDING THAT CHANGES THE PLAN, AND IT IS A WRONG PREMISE IN THE EXEMPTION\u0027S OWN REASON.  Measured, not reasoned. ***  All three Alpha entries read \u0027no gate runs an Alpha machine; gate_crossfamily.sh is the named home and giving it one is a harness round\u0027.  That premise is TRUE OF RIGS AND FALSE OF PROBES.  gate_crossfamily.sh (gate 5) drives BOOTABLE RIG IMAGES through drive_guest.py, so \u0027an Alpha leg\u0027 reads as \u0027obtain a bootable Alpha guest\u0027, which genuinely is a harness round.  But measured against the probes themselves: NONE of the three references a disk image at all; each execs `-E alpha\u0027 as a rung-3 COLD-DEBUGGER probe; and each already emits the parseable token a gate scores (ALPHATBI_RESULT, LCABADVAL_RESULT, LCAISALANE_RESULT).  gate_arm.sh already wires probes in exactly this shape -- `python3 \u003cprobe\u003e.py \"$BIN\" \u003e \"$LOG\" 2\u003e\u00261 || true\u0027 followed by count rows -- and runs SIX of them.  So what these three need is a PROBE SECTION, not an Alpha rig: roughly six lines apiece in an idiom the tree already uses six times.  ONE gate run discharges all three.  THE STAGGER, therefore: WIRE the three Alpha probes into gate 5 as a probe section (they also fit its stated purpose -- \u0027non-MIPS CPU cores actually execute guest code\u0027 -- since a cold-debugger probe executing real Alpha instructions is precisely that).  RE-DATE the other eight to separated deadlines by CLASS, so the next cliff cannot re-form: the four wdc_* together behind the single-leg-mode instrument change, fbpending_drain behind EXPECT_CAP, mk48_nvram behind the mvme187 leg, ppc_rldicl on its own and longest, since it is blocked by a do-not-commit constraint rather than by work.  NOT DONE IN THIS TURN, DELIBERATELY: the exemption block states that each wiring owes ONE CLEAN GATE RUN proving it green before it ships, citing FOUR probes that shipped latently RED because the commit adding them never fired the gate.  A gate run contends with the #531 measurement in flight, so the wiring lands with its gate run and not before.  The deletion rule is already recorded here and is followed: a wired probe that KEEPS its exemption is a STALE EXEMPTION and this checker reds on it, so each entry is DELETED in the SAME edit that adds its wiring.",
        "entries":  [
                        {
                            "seat":  "codex",
