regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:8:   "new": "The trap in that fact is narrower than a blind spot: a decode that FORGETS the swap is caught\nby any inexact directed-mode row on either sign (the mutant table below reds three positive-only\nrows), but a single red is shared with other wrong stores -- a truncating store also reds\n`fadd-pos toward+Inf`.  What is unique to the missing swap is the SIGNATURE: with the same\nmagnitudes at both signs, all four directed rows go red together, because toward+Inf then\ntruncates the positive sum and grows the negative one, where a truncating store reds two of the\nfour and a nearest-only store the other two.  Gate 11 therefore runs sign-asymmetric pairs, the\nsame magnitudes with the sign flipped, and asserts four negative-operand tripwire rows by name:\n`fadd-neg toward+Inf`, `fadd-neg toward-Inf`, `flt-neg toward-Inf`, `flt-neg toward+Inf`. [#560]",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:16:   "new": "the MIPS Integer-Overflow *trap*: all six handlers contain\noverflow detection. The ADD/ADDI/DADD/DADDI predicates are correct when their handlers execute; SUB/DSUB\npredicates fail for a minimum signed subtrahend. Zero-destination decoding separately suppresses these handlers\nand their overflow exceptions. [#560]",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:24:   "new": "all six handlers contain overflow detection. The ADD/ADDI/DADD/DADDI\n> predicates are correct when their handlers execute; SUB/DSUB predicates fail for a minimum signed subtrahend.\n> Zero-destination decoding separately suppresses these handlers and their overflow exceptions. [#560]",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:32:   "new": "3. The guards are NOT redundant, and for TLB_A/B/C and TEX they are\n\t *     not modelling fidelity only: the store loop consumes TWO bytes per\n\t *     iteration (`data[hwi]*256 + data[hwi+1]'), so a len == 1 access\n\t *     would read `data[1]', one byte past the caller's buffer (for the\n\t *     MIPS generic load/store, `data[LS_SIZE]' in\n\t *     cpu_mips_instr_loadstore.c), and the width guard is a memory-safety\n\t *     check there too (OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:14809-14812).  For LINEAR_A and\n\t *     LINEAR_B the store is fixed-arity; see the note at those two sites\n\t *     (#560).",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:40:   "new": "`assert = 0' -> `return' at THIS line\n\t    passes the first seven rows (killtable_20260908/M_return.raw, rows\n\t    genctl ctl..c040 predirty all ok; it was not run in table 1), but the\n\t    `c040 noassert' / `c040 noassert2' rows that shipped with this comment\n\t    start with the line ALREADY ASSERTED, so the `return', which skips the\n\t    INTERRUPT_DEASSERT below, leaves it asserted and reddens them: KILLED\n\t    9/10 (killtable_20260908/M_return.raw) and 14/16 on the repaired probe\n\t    (ctlq_20260909/repaired_M_return.raw) (#560).)",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:48:   "new": "**DISPOSITION 3 (file as a new item) was chosen on the belief that NOBODY HAD MEASURED IT, and that\nbelief was false:** the `return' variant had already been built and KILLED, 9/10 by `c040 noassert'\n(killtable_20260908/M_return.raw, #512 table 2) and 14/16 on the repaired probe\n(ctlq_20260909/repaired_M_return.raw). The seats' prediction is a re-derivation of a measured, killed\nmutant, not an open gap. [#560]",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:56:   "new": "(That `return' variant IS the one dev_pcc2.c's comment calls 'a one-token mutant every shipped row\n#  passes'; that comment was false when it shipped -- the same commit, 9fa07fe9, shipped `c040 noassert',\n#  which kills it 9/10 (killtable_20260908/M_return.raw).) (#560)",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:64:   "new": "#274: The descriptor is owned by the chip, but it is not\n\t\t *  usable.  The lookahead below only lets a frame continue\n\t\t *  into a descriptor that this very test accepted AT LOOKAHEAD\n\t\t *  TIME, so within one call this is the FIRST buffer of a\n\t\t *  frame: nothing has been filled in, and the descriptor is\n\t\t *  deliberately NOT written back.  Drop the frame just like\n\t\t *  the exhaustion case above.  One caveat: the full-ring exit\n\t\t *  at :663-667 returns with a partial frame retained\n\t\t *  (le_register_fix keeps it, :767-770), so if a descriptor\n\t\t *  changes before a LATER call, that call can reach this arm\n\t\t *  mid-frame; it then drops the partial frame with MISS\n\t\t *  (#560).  (The manual gives no status for a descriptor\n\t\t *  violating the '1111' rule; reporting it as a missed packet\n\t\t *  is emulator policy, not documented Am7990 behaviour.)",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:72:   "new": "M8 (`|=` -> `=` on the assert arm)\nmeasured EQUIVALENT on #516's detector, and the warrant is time-indexed: at #516 `warned` held only the\ntwo IRQ bits, so the first assert -- the only one that assigns -- saw `warned == 0`.  Since #518 the same\nword carries six IOC announce bits (dev_lca.c:53-60, written at :331 and :495), so an IOC announce before\nthe first ISA interrupt makes `=` erase those latches: M8 is NOT equivalent at HEAD (whether #516's table\nwould red on a re-run depends on row order and is not measured).  The aggregate-edge reasoning\n(`irr & ~ier`) is unchanged and still right [#560]: the first assert is the only one that assigns because\nthe stub is entered only on edges of the AGGREGATE `pic1->irr & ~pic1->ier`, which is 0 until the first\nassert -- NOT because",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:80:   "new": "The source's \"aren't used yet\" TODO (`cpu_arm.c:163`) is about the iway/dway values, not the\nregister. A guest reads the Cache Type register directly (MRC p15,0,Rd,c0,c0,1;\n`cpu_arm_coproc.c`'s case 1 returns `cachetype`). So the change is guest-visible on every CPU whose\ntable entry leaves a cache size unspecified -- for ARM1136JSR1 (rpi), 0xffdea0ea becomes 0x0b02a0ea.\n[#560]",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:88:   "new": "because the interpreter requires both an odd `pc` and\n`ARM_FLAG_T` in `cpsr` (`cpu_arm.c:1216`); the probe's BX establishes both conditions\ntogether in one guest instruction. [#560]",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:96:   "new": "Applied UNCONDITIONALLY rather than gated on architecture version.\n\t *  Rotation is the architected result for ARMv3/v4/v5 and for v6+ with\n\t *  U == 0, when alignment checking is off (A == 0). With A == 1 an\n\t *  alignment Data Abort is owed, and neither form raises it (A is enabled\n\t *  at reset and never enforced). For v6+ with U == 1 the architecture\n\t *  wants a true unaligned access, which neither masking nor\n\t *  masking-plus-rotation implements. Each returns the required word only\n\t *  by coincidence of the data, and on different data, so rotation is not\n\t *  'never worse' there. The unconditional choice rests on the U == 0\n\t *  cases and on gating being unsound (#560).",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:104:   "new": "Rotation is the architected result for ARMv3/v4/v5\nand for v6+ with `U == 0`, when alignment checking is off (`A == 0`). With `A == 1` an\nalignment Data Abort is owed, and neither form raises it (A is enabled at reset and never\nenforced). For v6+ with `U == 1` the architecture wants a true unaligned access, which\nneither masking nor masking-plus-rotation implements. Each returns the required word only by\ncoincidence of the data, and on different data, so rotation is not 'never worse' there. The\nunconditional choice rests on the `U == 0` cases and on gating being unsound. [#560]",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:112:   "new": "rotation is the architected result for ARMv3/v4/v5 and for v6+\n> with `U == 0`, when alignment checking is off (`A == 0`). With `A == 1` an alignment Data Abort is owed,\n> and neither form raises it (A is enabled at reset and never enforced). For v6+ with `U == 1` the\n> architecture wants a true unaligned access, which neither masking nor masking-plus-rotation implements.\n> Each returns the required word only by coincidence of the data, and on different data, so rotation is\n> not 'never worse' there. The unconditional choice rests on the `U == 0` cases and on gating being\n> unsound. [#560]",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:120:   "new": "five `exit(1)` sites warn and continue -- the counter-1 DMA-refresh TODO, a count WRITE and a count READ to a counter with no programmed RW format (the `default:` arms), BCD-mode selection, and the unimplemented-offset `default:`.  Four are guest-reachable; the fifth is not: the device registers offsets 0-3 and all four have cases (`pit8253flood` records it as dead code). [#560]",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:128:   "new": "**This corrects #439's stated reasoning where the two fixes meet.** #439's read arm took an\nearly `break` \"so no counter byte was consumed, leave the selector alone\".  Under the datasheet\na latched read DOES consume one of the format's bytes: in the two-byte format the read flip-flop\nadvances for it, and in a one-byte format the single read releases the latch.  Across a two-read\n`gettick()` entered at EVEN read parity the two designs agree (two toggles return the flip-flop to\nLSB: 0x0000 both); at ODD parity they do not -- the first read already has rw == MSB and releases\nthe latch, the second returns the live low byte, so this round reads 0x9c00 for the 0x2e9c example\nwhere #439 read 0x0000 (#439's arm returned 0 for both reads at either parity).  That is a measured\nchange, closer to the hardware; #439's reason for existing survives either way (the divisor is\nnon-zero in both).  Verified: R1/R2/R3, #439's own rows, stay green throughout.  [This paragraph\nfirst said the designs were indistinguishable and #439's measured behaviour unchanged -- false at\nodd parity, as the pass-2 note below records.] [#560]",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:136:   "new": "a PCI-config access decoding to bus 255 announces itself and returns handled instead of `exit(1)` -- without going through `bus_pci_data_access()` or the read writeback, so a read there returns the pre-zeroed buffer (0). At register 0 that differs from the model's no-device answer elsewhere (all ones for the vendor-ID word); at other registers the two coincide. Filed as `fbpcibus255zero`. [#560] |",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:144:   "new": "The model exposes the external\n\t\t    interrupt inputs, but its timers do not count their assertion edges;\n\t\t    external mode instead substitutes the internal timer cadence (#560).\n\t\t    Modelling the external event properly is filed.  */",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:152:   "new": "footbridge_effective_cycles() substitutes the\n\t\t\t    unprescaled internal period for the external clock --\n\t\t\t    a defined, survivable, documented-divergent meaning;\n\t\t\t    the datasheet exists now (_scratchpad/dc21285.txt:\n\t\t\t    4124-4127) and the modelling is filed as\n\t\t\t    fbexternalclock (#560).",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:160:   "new": "The model exposes the external interrupt\n  inputs, but its timers do not count their assertion edges; external mode instead substitutes the\n  internal timer cadence. [#560]",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:168:   "new": "The choice rests on failure asymmetry alone (wrong-and-noisy beats wrong-and-silent: a\nguest that gets a few unwanted interrupts limps visibly, a guest waiting on a tick that never\ncomes hangs at boot).  The arithmetic support this sentence first carried -- that the 24-bit mask\naliases a legitimate write of `0x1000000` to zero and full-period keeps the rate continuous across\nthe register's own truncation -- was WITHDRAWN against the datasheet (7.3.39: TimerNLoad bits 31:24\nread only as 0, so bit 24 is outside the writable field and there is no wrap to be continuous\nacross); the source comment records the withdrawal, this paragraph did not. [#560]",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:176:   "new": "Show the store (writer pc, target,\n\t *  width, value) and request debugger entry when the emulation loop\n\t *  next consumes about_to_enter_single_step; further guest instructions\n\t *  may execute before entry (#560). NOP unless a watchpoint is set.",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:184:   "new": "Zero the rest here: the failed\n\t\t\t\t    chunk and everything after it.  Bytes already read by\n\t\t\t\t    earlier chunks remain, so #244's whole-buffer zero is not\n\t\t\t\t    fully restored (#560).",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:192:   "new": "The wrapper now zeroes the remainder. **[#560, 2026-10-06]** The\nwrapper zeroes the failed chunk and everything after it; bytes already read by earlier chunks remain,\nso #244's whole-buffer zero is not fully restored.",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:200:   "new": "one boundary\nper 1,024 split granules [#560]",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:208:   "new": "Without that exemption an aligned 16 MiB transfer would require 16,384 worker calls at the\n  1 KiB granule [#560];",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:216:   "new": "(The worker's host-page\n *  granule is 8 KB under MEM_ALPHA and 4 KB otherwise; the split granule is 1 KB (#560).)",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:224:   "new": "post-#430 it can be reached by PHYSICAL accesses or when translate_v2p\n *      is NULL; translated accesses are split at the 1 KB granule first (#560).\n *      A TRANSLATED 4096-byte write across a 1 MB boundary is chunked at the\n *      1024-byte SPLIT granule, so it costs 4 translations",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:232:   "new": "The **override semantics are deliberately unchanged** (panel-unanimous); the listing now states them explicitly. ARC's NE2000 transmit of an unimplemented IPv4 broadcast reaches `debugmsg(SUBSYS_NET, ..., VERBOSITY_ERROR)` through `net_ethernet_tx`. The caller gate is `ENOUGH_VERBOSITY(SUBSYS_NET, VERBOSITY_ERROR)`, and the default verbosity is INFO (2), so the call is made. Clearing the net breakpoint does not clear `debugmsg_break_on_error`, so the global override still requests debugger entry. pmax's LANCE (`dev_le.c:438`) reaches the same path through `net_ethernet_tx`. [#560]",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:240:   "new": "**#42** prefer `/bin/gunzip` and `/usr/bin/gunzip`, with a PATH lookup fallback and the inherited environment retained, plus the `fd != STDOUT_FILENO` close guard and the `--` option-injection guard [#560]",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:248:   "new": "/*  #177: the request debug dump's fixed reads stop at packet[33];\n\t    bytes from 34 are logged only while i < len.  The later field\n\t    dump, including packet[85], runs after packet = lp->data on the\n\t    307-byte reply.  Frames shorter than 278 bytes are rejected\n\t    before the memcpy of the request.  The len < 86 guard does not\n\t    protect a fixed request read of packet[85] (#560).  */",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:256:   "new": "`mmap` vs `MAP_FAILED`, DHCP debug over-read).\n**[#560, 2026-10-06]** Annotation at 8ea26abb: the DHCP debug over-read clause for #177 does not match the dumps\nin that tree. The request dump's fixed reads stop at packet[33] and then use i < len. packet[85] is read only\nafter packet = lp->data, on the allocated reply. At 39748e3c the text packet[85] does not occur in\nsrc/net/net_ip.c; at de1398e1a the only occurrence is this comment.",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:264:   "new": "/*  #132: a full ARP/RARP body is 28 bytes.  The callers guarantee\n\t    an Ethernet-frame length of at least 20 bytes; after removing the\n\t    14-byte Ethernet header, this function may receive a 6-byte\n\t    body.  Reject bodies shorter than 28 bytes before fixed-field\n\t    accesses or debug output (#560).  */",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:272:   "new": "loud-once `fatal()`/first-N on each previously-silent OOB skip/zero/clamp **[#560, 2026-10-06]** Superseded in net/net.c by 5ee491e7: the ARP (net.c:195) and RARP (net.c:221) paths each use one WARNING-level debugmsg, and the once-only latch is set after the call returns even when debugmsg prints nothing (verbosity below WARNING, no subsystem breakpoint, not single-stepping). |",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:280:   "new": "and route the pending-tick assert through\n  `jazz_timer_irq`. **[#560, 2026-10-06]** Superseded by 9d18d156: at 8ea26abb, JAZZ pending-line bits and\n  R4030 EXT_IMASK CPU-IP enable bits occupy separate namespaces. Timer pending is 0x8000; its IP6 enable is\n  0x10. IRQ3 requires a pending local-bus line and EXT_IP3 (0x02); IRQ6 requires pending timer line 15 and\n  EXT_IP6 (0x10). EXT_IMASK writes recompute these predicates, and timer delivery uses jazz_timer_irq.",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:288:   "new": "(the resulting address is passed to the guest physical-memory dispatcher; the DMA controller does not confine it to RAM, and this observation does not establish a host buffer overrun) [#560]",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:296:   "new": "ARC's missing-handler and ordinary unsupported-vector state\n  dumps require SUBSYS_PROMEMUL verbosity VERBOSITY_DEBUG; a promemul subsystem breakpoint alone does not\n  enable them [#560].",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:301:   "id": "hrec 1 hbundle_regress13:F9",
regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_560.json:304:   "new": "So no row here can observe the split -- and, worse, \"bnd lo\" (a0 = 1,\n    ITB) would go RED ON A CORRECT IMPLEMENTATION of it: it observes X with an\n    ordinary DATA load, so a real ITB-only flush leaves that load reading the\n    stale value.  \"bnd mid\" (a0 = 2, DTB) stays green.  A future round\n    implementing the split MUST re-pin \"bnd lo\" FIRST and measure it red\n    before changing the code, per the oracle-correction rule (#560).",
